(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ocular-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced Ocular History & Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a trauma patient, what must NOT be done?","choices":["Do not take a history","Do not palpate the globe","Do not measure visual acuity","Do not inspect the lids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — in trauma it is critical not to palpate the globe, and too much pressure on a ruptured globe is a known mistake. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which two tests together make up the best confrontation field examination?","choices":["Tonometry and fundoscopy","The static finger wiggle test and the kinetic red target test","The swinging light test together with the cover-uncover test","The pinhole test and the Snellen chart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confrontation fields are best tested by combining the static finger wiggle test with the kinetic red target test. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which three features define Horner syndrome?","choices":["A large slow pupil with reduced light reaction and a slow near reaction","Ptosis, miosis of the affected eye, anhidrosis of the ipsilateral face","Ptosis, mydriasis, lateral deviation, and a fixed pupil","Lid lag, proptosis, chemosis and diplopia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Horner syndrome gives ptosis, miosis and ipsilateral facial anhidrosis, and the small pupil still reacts to light and near. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Entropion and ectropion both affect which eyelid?","choices":["Neither — they affect the conjunctiva","The lower lid","The upper lid","Both lids equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — entropion is inward folding and ectropion outward turning, and both affect the lower lid. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What causes SENILE ptosis?","choices":["Compression of the oculomotor nerve","Weakened muscle, relaxed tissues","Antibody blockade at the neuromuscular junction of the levator","Interruption of the sympathetic supply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — senile ptosis comes from weakened muscle and relaxed tissues, plus the weight of herniated fat. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why may a zygomatic fracture cause pain on opening the mouth?","choices":["The masseter originates on the mandible and inserts on the zygoma","The infraorbital nerve is compressed during jaw movement","The temporomandibular joint is always involved","The temporalis muscle passes medial to the arch and inserts on the mandible"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the temporalis passes medial to the zygomatic arch and inserts on the mandible, so a fracture can hurt on opening. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Watery or mucoid discharge suggests which conditions?","choices":["Acute glaucoma","Allergic or viral","Bacterial infection","Acute iritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — watery or mucoid discharge goes with allergic or viral conditions, while purulent discharge suggests bacteria. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A temporal field defect in one eye should prompt what?","choices":["Testing for a nasal defect in the other eye","Immediate fundoscopy of the same eye only","Repeating acuity in the same eye","Measuring intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a temporal defect in one eye may suggest a nasal defect in the other eye, so test that possibility. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is interrupted in Horner syndrome?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve","The optic nerve","The sympathetic innervation of the pupil and the levator palpebrae superioris","The parasympathetic supply travelling through the ciliary ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — interrupting sympathetic supply to the pupil and levator palpebrae superioris explains both the miosis and the ptosis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"After a chemical or fluid splash to the eye, what must you establish?","choices":["The manufacturer of the product","The acidity or alkalinity of the fluid","The volume of fluid involved","Whether the patient was wearing contact lenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — after any chemical or fluid exposure, knowing whether the fluid was acidic or alkaline is essential. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two conditions are associated with blepharitis?","choices":["Hypothyroidism and iron deficiency","Graves disease and myasthenia gravis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Bacterial infection and atopic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — blepharitis is a common eye inflammation associated with bacterial infections and with atopic dermatitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the rule about topical anesthetic in corneal abrasion?","choices":["It should be prescribed for home use to control the pain","It should never be used at all","It is only useful if the cornea is hazy","Use it for diagnosis, not as a treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical anesthetic brings immediate relief of an abrasion, but it is used for diagnosis and not as a treatment. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Injection localized JUST AROUND THE CORNEA suggests which three conditions?","choices":["Keratitis, iritis, or acute glaucoma","Conjunctivitis, blepharitis, or dry eye","Subconjunctival hemorrhage, pinguecula, or pterygium","Episcleritis, chalazion, or hordeolum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — injection just around the cornea could result from keratitis, iritis or acute glaucoma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the warning about pressure on a globe that may be ruptured?","choices":["Placing too much pressure on it is a mistake","Pressure is required to assess the depth of injury","Pressure is harmless once a topical anesthetic has been instilled","Pressure should be applied only with a cotton swab"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — failing to diagnose a ruptured globe and placing too much pressure on it are both common examination mistakes. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cause of red eye shows redness that is DIFFUSE and maximal peripherally, with vision unaffected?","choices":["Glaucoma","Corneal injury or infection","Conjunctivitis","Acute iritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — conjunctivitis gives diffuse conjunctival redness maximal peripherally, with vision affected only by mild blurring. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Why does moving the light to the affected eye cause BOTH pupils to dilate in an afferent defect?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve is being compressed on that side","The ciliary ganglion has degenerated","The afferent stimulus on that side is reduced","The affected pupil is pharmacologically blocked"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the reduced afferent stimulus reduces the efferent signals to both pupils, so a net dilation occurs. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Deep eye pain is associated with which condition?","choices":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Corneal abrasion","Blepharitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — deep eye pain is linked to acute narrow angle glaucoma; pain from a deeper source is not relieved by topical anesthetic. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which of these complaints should be referred to ophthalmology IMMEDIATELY?","choices":["Hyphema","Orbital cellulitis","Corneal abrasion","Chemical burn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chemical burns are emergent and go to ophthalmology immediately, as do rupture and sudden vision loss. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Lateral sparseness of the eyebrows suggests what?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Seborrheic dermatitis","Iron deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lateral sparseness of the eyebrows is the eyebrow finding associated with hypothyroidism. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides Horner syndrome and the senile and congenital forms, which causes of ptosis remain?","choices":["Myasthenia gravis, oculomotor nerve damage","Bell palsy, trigeminal neuralgia and stroke","Thyroid eye disease, orbital cellulitis and orbital tumor","Blepharitis, chalazion and dermatochalasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ptosis also comes from myasthenia gravis and oculomotor nerve damage, alongside Horner syndrome's sympathetic loss. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pair of diagnoses must be differentiated because the latter can lead to death?","choices":["Episcleritis from scleritis","Cataract from glaucoma","Hordeolum from chalazion","Preseptal from orbital cellulitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — preseptal cellulitis must be told apart from orbital cellulitis, because orbital cellulitis can lead to death. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In acute glaucoma, what is the pupil like?","choices":["May be small and, with time, irregular","Not affected unless iritis develops","Dilated and fixed","Not affected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — acute glaucoma gives a dilated, fixed pupil, with a steamy cornea and an acute rise in intraocular pressure. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which examination comes FIRST in the suggested order?","choices":["Direct ophthalmoscopy","Ocular pressure","Inspection","Visual acuity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the suggested order begins with inspection, then the external examination, before acuity and fields. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What should you do if nystagmus is seen on lateral gaze?","choices":["Refer immediately regardless","Measure the intraocular pressure","Bring the finger back into binocular vision","Repeat the whole test with the other eye covered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bring the finger back into binocular vision; nystagmus that persists there suggests a neurologic condition. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"When should the lacrimal sac compression test be avoided?","choices":["If the patient has previously had cataract surgery on that eye","If the patient is over sixty-five","If the area is significantly inflamed or tender","If the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lacrimal sac compression test is avoided if the area is significantly inflamed or tender. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A BLUE sclera indicates what?","choices":["Long-term steroid use","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Liver disease","Thyroid eye disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a blue sclera is the scleral color seen in osteogenesis imperfecta. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Eye pain with PHOTOPHOBIA suggests what?","choices":["Inflammation of the iris","Optic neuritis","Temporal arteritis","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — eye pain accompanied by photophobia is the pairing for inflammation of the iris. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the cover-uncover test reveal?","choices":["A slight muscle imbalance not otherwise seen","An afferent pupillary defect of the optic nerve","A refractive error","A field defect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the cover-uncover test may reveal a slight muscle imbalance not otherwise seen. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In acute glaucoma, what is the cornea like?","choices":["Steamy and cloudy","Clear","Clear or slightly clouded","Changes depending on the cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — acute glaucoma makes the cornea steamy and cloudy, alongside a dilated fixed pupil and severe deep pain. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"What is the first step in everting the upper lid to search for a foreign body?","choices":["Ask the patient to look up and relax, then pull the upper lid directly upward","Press on the globe itself to push the lid outward","Use a cotton swab placed at the lid margin itself","Ask the patient to look down and relax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the patient looks down and relaxes; then the lashes are pulled down and forward over a stick placed 1 cm above the margin. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
